From: KRdp displayless experiment <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] Do not delete client GFX objects after a caps re-advertisement

A client may advertise its GFX caps a second time within a connection, and by
then it has reset its graphics state: its surfaces and encoding contexts are
already gone. mstsc does so when it rebuilds its graphics pipeline, for instance
after falling back from a hardware to a software framebuffer.
KRdp treated that as a channel reset but still sent DeleteEncodingContext and
DeleteSurface for the objects it had created, which mstsc rejects with
E_INVALIDARG and answers by dropping the connection with a protocol error
(disconnect reason 3334). It only matters when a frame went out before the
second advertisement.

Drop the surface on the server side only in that case. Surface ids are never
reused, so even a client that kept its old surface sees no collision.

diff --git a/src/VideoStream.cpp b/src/VideoStream.cpp
index 4ed5c41..e2718c9 100644
--- a/src/VideoStream.cpp
+++ b/src/VideoStream.cpp
@@ -557,7 +557,12 @@ uint32_t VideoStream::onCapsAdvertise(const RDPGFX_CAPS_ADVERTISE_PDU *capsAdver
         qCDebug(KRDP) << "GFX channel reset (re-advertisement), resetting surface state";
         d->capsConfirmed = false;
         d->surface->pendingReset = true;
-        destroySurface();
+        // A client re-advertising its caps has reset its whole GFX state, so its
+        // surfaces and encoding contexts are gone already. Telling it to delete
+        // them anyway is a protocol error to mstsc: it rejects DeleteEncodingContext
+        // with E_INVALIDARG and drops the connection (reason 3334). Forget them
+        // on this side only.
+        destroySurface(false);
         {
             std::lock_guard lock(d->pendingFramesMutex);
             d->pendingFrames.clear();
@@ -722,14 +728,14 @@ bool VideoStream::openChannel()
     return true;
 }
 
-void VideoStream::destroySurface()
+void VideoStream::destroySurface(bool notifyClient)
 {
     auto &surface = d->surface->surface;
     if (surface.id == 0) {
         return;
     }
 
-    if (d->gfxContext && surface.codecContextId != 0) {
+    if (notifyClient && d->gfxContext && surface.codecContextId != 0) {
         RDPGFX_DELETE_ENCODING_CONTEXT_PDU deleteEncodingContextPdu = {};
         deleteEncodingContextPdu.surfaceId = surface.id;
         deleteEncodingContextPdu.codecContextId = surface.codecContextId;
@@ -739,7 +745,7 @@ void VideoStream::destroySurface()
         }
     }
 
-    if (d->gfxContext) {
+    if (notifyClient && d->gfxContext) {
         RDPGFX_DELETE_SURFACE_PDU deleteSurfacePdu = {};
         deleteSurfacePdu.surfaceId = surface.id;
         const UINT status = d->gfxContext->DeleteSurface(d->gfxContext.get(), &deleteSurfacePdu);
diff --git a/src/VideoStream.h b/src/VideoStream.h
index 1ec840f..691032e 100644
--- a/src/VideoStream.h
+++ b/src/VideoStream.h
@@ -110,7 +110,9 @@ private:
     void setSize(const QSize &size);
     bool streamingEnabled() const;
     void failVideoInitialization();
-    void destroySurface();
+    /// Tear down the GFX surface. With notifyClient false only the server-side state is
+    /// dropped, for when the client has already discarded its own.
+    void destroySurface(bool notifyClient = true);
     void performReset(QSize size);
     bool hasInFlightCapacity() const;
     void sendFrame(const VideoFrame &frame);
